+#### 11.1.2.1 …and on `kern_big` it is one blit
+
+The box is a 13×13 picture — the outer square filled and framed, the large
+square framed, the small one filled and framed — and `wm_grow_paint` drew it as
+two `gfx_fill`s and three `gfx_frame`s: **fourteen primitive calls**, ~49k
+cycles, 10 ms on the 5150/CGA, for 169 pixels, after every draw of a front
+sizable window and every package repaint that asks for it. On `kern_big` the
+picture those calls leave is a 13-word bitmap (`wm_grow_bmp`); it is shifted to
+the box's pixel phase into a 39-byte band (`wm_gr_band`, three bytes a row,
+`x & 7` pixels in — where `gfx_blit1` starts an off-grid band, §5.4.2.8) and
+put down in ONE `gfx_blit1`, in `thm_pair`'s ink and ground: §5.4.2.2's pen on
+a VGA, and on a 1bpp adapter, which reads no pen, the band turned over when the
+ground is the lit colour (Bright's and Windows' white). The pen is put back
+after the call, because the lock hold may blit again.
+
+Two cases keep the fourteen calls, because `gfx_blit1` is greying-blind by
+contract (§5.4.2) and a fill is not: a DISABLED pen (`[gfx_dis]`) and a window
+that declared two colours (`[gfx_mono1]`), which `font_ink` reduces. The
+Windows theme's grip is WIN95.DRV's (`THV_GRIP`) and is untouched; `kern_small`
+has no off-grid blit and keeps the calls.
+
